  "strategy": "We propose to use a novel representation of the step function h(x) and optimize it using a gradient-based method on a continuous representation. The key idea is to parametrize the step function using reflection control points, allowing smooth optimization. This approach is different from the previous tent function and genetic algorithm methods:\n\n1. Approach and rationale:\n   - Represent h(x) using 4 reflection control points (c1, c2, c3, c4) that define piecewise linear segments\n   - Interpolate between control points to construct a continuous function\n   - Project the continuous function onto a step function by thresholding at optimally chosen levels\n   - The control points are evolved using gradient descent to minimize the maximum overlap\n   - This method is more efficient than previous tent function methods because it directly optimizes the reflection points\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n   - Representation: h(x) = interpolate(x, c1, c2, c3, c4) where interpolation is piecewise linear\n   - Objective: min max_k \u222b[0,2] h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n   - Constraints: h(x) \u2208 [0,1] for all x, \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)dx=1\n   - We optimize over reflection points and threshold levels simultaneously\n\n3. Implementation:\n   - Define helper functions for continuous interpolation, projection to step function, and feasibility check\n   - Initialize with reflection points uniformly distributed in [0,2]\n   - Use gradient descent with line search\n   - Repair function maintains the integral constraint by adjusting thresholds\n   - Requires custom gradient computation for the objective\n   - Includes early termination to stay within budget\n\n4. Search choices:\n   - 4 reflection points (fixed)\n   - Optimization with projection\n   - Allowed search directions: reflection point adjustments and threshold selection\n   - Maximum iterations: 200 (capped by budget)\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n   - Dominant costs: gradient computation using finite differences\n   - Estimated time for 200 iterations: 15-20 seconds on a single CPU\n   - Capped by budget_s parameter\n   - Graceful degradation at runtime boundary\n   - Automatic restart with new initialization if progress stalls\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n   - Extract feasibility, sum constraint satisfaction, and continuous projection\n   - Re-evaluate final solution after projection\n   - Ensure binary properties after projection\n   - Return (projected_h, c5_bound, n_points)",
